============================================================
PART A — the seven reworked tests themselves: NO DEFECT FOUND.
============================================================
I tried hard to break these and could not. Recording this in full because a
PASS-shaped result on this part should not be mistaken for a PASS overall
(see PART B, which is why the verdict is FAIL).

A1. Wrong-reason-failure attack, traced against production source, not test
    prose:
  - storage (internal/services/storage/migration.go:451
    `rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient`): the loop's FIRST operation on each
    path is `os.ReadFile(path)`, no earlier Stat/Lstat/walk that could skip a
    dangling symlink. A symlink to a nonexistent target fails exactly there
    (ENOENT), landing on `failed = append(failed, path); continue` — the same
    branch chmod-0000 used to force before root bypassed it. Confirmed for
    BOTH reworked tests (TestRollbackDecryptionWithRecipientReportsUnrestorable
    Files and TestRollbackDecryptionReportsFullPaths — the second one was not
    in NEXT.md's original inventory but was reworked too, a bonus fix).
  - curate delete (delete.go:118-124) and upsert (upsert.go:179-181): both
    read `deps.Snapshots.Ensure(f, now)`, which is `snapshot.go:75
    os.ReadFile(src)` with NO earlier existence check of its own. Swapping
    the file for an empty directory makes that ReadFile fail with EISDIR
    (confirmed via the test's own failure text when mutated, see A3 below:
    "read .../major_expenses.json: is a directory"). EISDIR does not satisfy
    `errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist)`, so it lands on the abort/skip branch
    the two tests defend, not the tolerated "file doesn't exist yet" branch a
    dangling-symlink injection would have hit instead. This is the exact
    concern flagged in the task brief and it holds.
  - explorer upload (handlers.go `createExclusive`, storage.go:513-517):
    `dir := filepath.Dir(path); os.MkdirAll(dir, 0755)` — Go's MkdirAll does
    an explicit `Stat` + `IsDir()` check and returns `&PathError{Err:
    syscall.ENOTDIR}` when the path exists as a non-directory, a logic check
    with no permission dependency root can bypass. Confirmed this reaches the
    SAME outcome branch as the original chmod test: handlers.go:932-935 maps
    any non-`os.ErrExist` error from `store.CreateExclusive` to
    `Status: "rejected", Reason: "error saving file"` — identical to what the
    old 0555-directory fixture produced for non-root (CreateTemp EACCES).
  - explorer delete (`storage.go:672 removeLocked` → plain `os.Remove`, NOT
    RemoveAll): a non-empty directory placeholder makes `os.Remove` fail with
    ENOTEMPTY, a kernel invariant (VFS unlink refuses a non-empty directory)
    no uid bypasses. `store.Stat` earlier in handlers.go:970 still finds the
    directory (not ErrNotExist), so the code reaches the exact `store.Remove`
    call and 500 branch the test defends.
  - explorer import ENAMETOOLONG (255-byte NAME_MAX basename): verified
    NAME_MAX=255 empirically on BOTH the container's actual /tmp (ext4,
    `getconf NAME_MAX /tmp` = 255) and on tmpfs (/dev/shm probe: a 255-byte
    name creates fine, a 265-byte one — the same name plus the real
    `StagingSuffix + <5-digit random>` used by `createExclusive` — fails
    ENAMETOOLONG). Confirmed this hits the same `deps.write` non-ErrExist
    branch as the original 0555-directory fixture (rejected / "could not
    save the file"), not some earlier or later branch.

A2. Assertion weakening: full `git diff` of all four files read in full.
    Only chmod/cleanup lines were replaced with kernel-enforced setup/
    restore; every assertion, every `t.Fatalf`/`t.Errorf` check, is
    unchanged; the one root-only `t.Skip` (in
    TestHandleImport_FailedWriteKeepsSource) was removed, not added; no test
    renamed; no `t.Skip` newly introduced anywhere in these four files
    (grepped: the only remaining Skips are a pre-existing Windows guard and
    two pre-existing symlink-support guards, none in the seven reworked
    tests, none uid-conditioned).

A3. Mutants the worker did not try, constructed and run myself in throwaway
    copies (isolated from the main tree):
  - storage: dropped the `failed = append(failed, path)` line entirely on
    the ReadFile-error branch → BOTH storage tests FAIL as designed
    ("expected failed=[...], got []" / "want 1 unrestored file, got 0").
  - curate delete: widened the tolerance to swallow ANY Ensure error (not
    just fs.ErrNotExist) → TestDeleteAbortsWhenAnExistingFileCannotBeBackedUp
    FAILS ("expected an explicit refusal ... got: read .../major_expenses.
    json: is a directory") — this also independently proves the injected
    errno is EISDIR, not ErrNotExist, confirming A1.
  - curate upsert: neutered the pin-skip-note guard → FAILS ("expected a
    note explaining the pin was skipped").
  - explorer delete: `_ = store.Remove(filePath)` (swallow the error) →
    FAILS ("expected 500 for remove error, got 200").
  - explorer upload: any CreateExclusive error mapped to "skipped" instead
    of "rejected" → FAILS ("expected status rejected, got {...Status:
    skipped}").
  - explorer import: a shallow "ignore write error, keep going" mutant
    (`_ = deps.write(...)`) surprisingly still PASSED — but this is not a
    rework regression: it passes because Step 5's independent readback
    check (`deps.readBack(destPath)`) also fails when nothing was written,
    landing on the same "rejected" outcome via a different guard — the
    SAME defense-in-depth existed under the original chmod fixture (an
    unwritable directory also makes the write no-op, and readback would
    equally fail). A faithful reconstruction of the mutation this test is
    actually built to catch — ignore BOTH the write error and the readback
    error, unconditionally proceed to delete the source — correctly FAILS
    ("a failed write must leave the source in place ... no such file or
    directory").
  All mutants killed except the one shown to be pre-existing and unrelated
  to the rework.

A4. Cross-uid, race, environment: ran the seven targeted tests, and the full
    `storage`/`curate`/`explorer` package suites, both as root (uid 0, this
    container's actual identity) and as `nobody` (uid 65534, via the NEXT.md
    `cp -a` + `chmod -R a+rwX` + isolated HOME/GOCACHE/GOPATH/GOMODCACHE +
    `su nobody` recipe) — all green both ways, including the full
    `storage` package (~120s, includes its slow race-relevant tests) and
    `curate`/`explorer` (fast, no race flag needed for stability there).
    `-race -count=3` on all seven, both uids: 100% PASS, zero flakes, zero
    `DATA RACE` warnings. No `t.Parallel` in any of the four files, so no
    fixture-path collision with parallel neighbors is possible.

============================================================
PART B — why the verdict is FAIL: the task's own Accept bar is not met.
============================================================
NEXT.md's T3 section states, verbatim: "**Accept**: full module `go test
./...` green AS ROOT and as uid≠0; each reworked test still kills its
original mutation at both uids." I ran exactly that command, as root, on a
clean `cp -a` copy of this branch (nohup, full log kept, no truncation):

  cd <copy> && go test ./... -count=1

Result: FAIL, in EIGHT packages, 49 individual `--- FAIL:` lines, NONE of
them in the three packages T3 touched (storage/curate/explorer all report
`ok`):
  FAIL  budget2/cmd/enrich-amazon            (TestRun_SaveEnrichmentError)
  FAIL  budget2/internal/config              (TestLoadUserSettingsPermissionError)
  FAIL  budget2/internal/handlers/backup     (TestHandleRestoreWriteError)
  FAIL  budget2/internal/handlers/majorexpenses (8 x *_SaveFails)
  FAIL  budget2/internal/handlers/whatif     (30 x *_SaveError / *_NonTypedError)
  FAIL  budget2/internal/services/retirement (8 x *_SaveFailure/*_SaveError/
                                               *_ReadError/*_RemoveError/
                                               *_WriteError/TestCRUD_SaveErrors)
Sample lines (grep -c "FAIL:" on the full log = 49):
  --- FAIL: TestRun_SaveEnrichmentError
  --- FAIL: TestLoadUserSettingsPermissionError
  --- FAIL: TestHandleRestoreWriteError
  --- FAIL: TestHandleAdd_SaveFails / TestHandleUpdate_SaveFails / ... (majorexpenses)
  --- FAIL: TestHandleWhatIfRothConversion_SaveError / ... (30 in whatif)
  --- FAIL: TestPurgeRemovedIncomeSource_SaveFailure / TestCRUD_SaveErrors / ... (retirement)
Re-ran the same full module suite as `nobody` (uid 65534): all of the above
pass (whatif 215s ok, retirement 247s ok, etc.) — confirming these are the
SAME defect class NEXT.md's "Environment facts #1" describes (a
chmod/read-only-directory fixture that root's CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE bypasses),
just in packages "Environment facts #1" never inventoried. (One unrelated
node-tooling failure, `internal/handlers/accounts`
TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness, appeared only in my nobody run
because my `su nobody` PATH omitted node; it is a PATH artifact of my own
harness, not a uid-permission defect, and is excluded from the count above.)

These 49 failures are pre-existing and untouched by this task (T3's diff
touches only the four *_test.go files in storage/curate/explorer, confirmed
by `git diff --stat` and `git diff internal/ | grep '^+++'`, matching
checker-tests' criterion 1) — they were already broken under root before
this branch existed, in code T3 never went near. That does not satisfy the
literal Accept line, though: "full module `go test ./...` green AS ROOT" is
false, by a wide margin (49 failing tests, 8 packages, six of which were
never mentioned in "Environment facts #1"'s inventory of "five... plus a
sixth"). checker-tests' PASS verdict never ran `go test ./...` at the module
level — its Criterion 2 command is scoped to
`./internal/services/storage/ ./internal/services/mcpsvc/curate/
./internal/handlers/explorer/` only, and its Criterion 3/4/6 stay within the
same three packages. That is a reasonable reading of "the affected
packages," but it is not what NEXT.md's Accept line says, and nothing in the
worker's report, the manifest, or the primary verdict documents this as a
deliberate, sign-off narrowing of scope.

Net effect: this is either (a) a real gate failure — the written Accept
criterion for T3 is unmet and "Environment facts #1" undercounted this
defect class by roughly 7x — or (b) a scope/documentation defect in NEXT.md
itself (the Accept line should have read "the three affected packages," to
match its own "Environment facts #1" inventory, and the newly-discovered ~49
tests belong in a new task). Either way, accepting T3 at this attempt
without the lead explicitly resolving which of (a)/(b) applies — and without
a corrected NEXT.md entry or a new task opened for the other ~8 packages —
would let a materially incomplete claim ("full module green") through the
gate unchallenged. Per my brief, I default to FAIL on this ambiguity rather
than silently accept the narrower scope checker-tests assumed.

============================================================
Distinguishing refutation from observation, per the brief's instruction:
============================================================
- REFUTATION (drives the FAIL): the literal, written T3 Accept criterion
  ("full module go test ./... green AS ROOT") is mechanically false, with
  reproducible evidence (49 `--- FAIL:` lines, full log retained at
  /tmp/claude-0/.../scratchpad/t3check/root-full.log during my run).
- OBSERVATIONS (not refutations, no defect found): every wrong-reason-
  failure attack (A1), assertion-weakening check (A2), constructed mutant
  (A3, five of six killed cleanly, the sixth's survival independently
  explained as pre-existing defense-in-depth rather than a rework
  regression), and cross-uid/race run (A4) on the seven reworked tests
  came back clean. If the task's scope is later clarified/ruled to be the
  three named packages only (matching "Environment facts #1"), PART A alone
  would support a PASS on the merits of the actual diff.
